response to the pandemic the clark  county school district is giving  eligible full-time employees a retention  bonus ccs ccsd trustees made the move  official during their regular board  meeting this week mary jane belliesa  shares how teachers are responding  this month the clark county school  district will pay full-time employees a  bonus of a thousand dollars and starting  in may ccsd will pay an additional one  thousand dollar bonus to full-time  employees vicki kreidel a second grade  teacher and president of the national  education alliance of southern nevada  says this money will help cover the  rising costs of bills during this  pandemic we've had an increase in  not just cost of living like rent and  everything is going up in vegas  but  our health costs have gone up  exponentially since october 1st  and  so  yeah that many will probably for most of  us be used in trying to cover some of  the additional expenses that we have  noelle clement is a teacher serving  students out in indian springs and is  part of the clark county education  association board she hopes the  eligibility guidelines doesn't exclude  staff who work the same amount of hours  but hold different positions and a lot  of it winds up being support positions  like our bus drivers and are in class  so these are also you know adult folks  that have specific skill sets and  they're working with kids and they may  not be getting the same  you know monetary support as full-time  teachers that are in the classroom ccsd  has allocated approximately 80 million  in elementary and secondary school  emergency relief funds to cover the  expected cost of the retention bonuses  mary jane balliesa live local now
 

